10\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:\n\nLooking at my situation:\n- I have only 2 cards left: ♥9 and ♦10\n- I need to beat ♦3 (a single card)\n- Both my cards can beat ♦3\n- Next Player has 13 cards, Prev Player has 7 cards\n\nLet me evaluate the options:\n\n1. **Play ♥9**: This beats ♦3 and leaves me with ♦10. If opponents pass, I win immediately. If they play, I still have ♦10 to potentially respond.\n\n2. **Play ♦10**: This beats ♦3 and leaves me with ♥9. Same logic as above but uses my higher card first.\n\n3. **Pass**: This keeps both cards but allows Prev Player to maintain control. This is risky as they only have 7 cards and could build momentum.\n\nKey considerations:\n- I\'m extremely close to winning with only 2 cards\n- The Prev Player (7 cards) is more threatening than Next Player (13 cards)\n- I should play aggressively to empty my hand\n- Playing the lower card (♥9) first is strategically better because:\n  - It conserves ♦10 as a stronger follow-up\n  - If opponents pass, I win\n  - If they respond, I still have ♦10 which can beat most single cards except J, Q, K, A, 2, and jokers\n\nReason:\n\nI should play ♥9 because I\'m in an excellent winning position with only 2 cards remaining. Playing the lower card first is the optimal strategy - it beats the ♦3 requirement while preserving my stronger ♦10 for the final play. Given that Prev Player only has 7 cards and has been playing actively, I cannot afford to pass and give them continued control. By playing ♥9, I either win immediately if both opponents pass, or I maintain pressure while keeping my higher card in reserve.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥9"]}'}
